-- title:
--   Order of the Hecke multiplier at a tame place
-- statement:
--   Let $k$ be a perfect field, $N\ge 1$ and $\ell$ a prime. Write $F_N=k(\bar\jmath(q),\bar\jmath(q^N))$ for the subfield `modularFunctionFieldC k N` of $k((q))$ generated by the two $j$-series, $j=$ `jGeomGen k N` for the generator $\bar\jmath(q)$ of $F_N$, and $R=$ `charLDegeneracyRoof k N ℓ` for the subfield of $k((q))$ generated by $\bar\jmath(q),\bar\jmath(q^N),\bar\jmath(q^\ell),\bar\jmath(q^{N\ell})$; let $\alpha=$ `heckeAlphaC k N ℓ` be the inclusion $F_N\hookrightarrow R$ and $\beta=$ `heckeBetaC k N ℓ` the $k$-algebra map induced by $q\mapsto q^{\ell}$. Assume: $\alpha$ and $\beta$ are integral ring maps (hypotheses $h\alpha$, $h\beta$); $F_N$ is algebraic over $k(j)$ and $R$ is finite over $k(\alpha j)$; an element $h\in R$ satisfies $d(\beta j)=h\cdot \alpha^{*}(dj)$ in $\Omega_{R/k}$, where $\alpha^{*}$ is `pullbackDiff`; $d(\alpha j)\neq 0$ and $d(\beta j)\neq 0$; and $y$ is a place of $R$ over $k$ (a proper valuation subring of $R$ containing $k$ and a principal ideal ring) whose ramification indices $e_\alpha,e_\beta$ along $\alpha$ and $\beta$ — the infima of the positive values $\operatorname{ord}_y(\varphi f)$, $f\neq0$ — are nonzero in $k$. Then $$\operatorname{ord}_y h=\bigl(e_\beta\cdot\operatorname{ord}^{\mathrm{diff}}_{y_\beta}(dj)+e_\beta\bigr)-\bigl(e_\alpha\cdot\operatorname{ord}^{\mathrm{diff}}_{y_\alpha}(dj)+e_\alpha\bigr),$$ where $y_\alpha,y_\beta$ are the restrictions of $y$ to $F_N$ along $\alpha,\beta$ and $\operatorname{ord}^{\mathrm{diff}}$ is the order of a differential, i.e. the order of its coefficient with respect to $d$ of a uniformizer.
--
--   This is the local computation of the divisor of the Hecke multiplier $h=d(\bar\jmath(q^\ell))/d(\bar\jmath(q))$ on the $\ell$-degeneracy roof above the level-$N$ modular function field, at places where both degeneracy maps are tamely ramified. It feeds the analysis of the special fibre in characteristic $\ell$, being used in the description of fibres and supersingular places and in the Riemann–Roch estimate for traces of $\beta$ against the weight divisor.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_ModularCurve_ord_heckeMultiplier_eq.lean

